  cites Build chapter PlugBuildPipeline

 A RUN DRIVEN BY EXIT CODES A REAL SCRIPT REALLY PRODUCED, over the pipeline the
 tree ACTUALLY SHIPS. The subject is `pb-pipe` from `codex/build/plugbuildPipeline`,
 cited rather than copied, so a stage renamed or a verdict edited in the generator
 moves this arm and nobody has to remember to keep a second copy in step.

 IT WAS A COPY UNTIL THE SPLIT. A chapter that declares `opening` can be cited by
 nothing (CDX3001, L-UNCITABLE), so no test could reach any generator's pipeline;
 the entry chapter now sits over a citable `PlugBuildPipeline`, and this is the
 first test pointed at a shipped pipeline rather than at a transcription of one.

 MEASURED AGAINST `build/plug-build.ps1` AT HEAD, 2026-09-09, by running it and
 by reading it:

   `plug-build.ps1 -PlugName nosuchplug` prints `MISSING plug directory` and
   EXITS 2, which is the code s04 declares.

   `exit 2` is the ONLY explicit exit in the shipped script. Success falls off
   the end after `Build-TranspilerPlug`, so the process exits 0 by TERMINATION
   and not by an `ScExit`.

 THAT SECOND FACT IS WHY SUCCESS IS `pipe-passed` AND NOT `pipe-exited 0`. The
 model reads a stage that falls through as "the stage did not exit", which is the
 onward edge; asking the run to explain an exit of 0 would ask it to explain a
 code the script never returns. A reader's first instinct is the other way round,
 which is why the two are separate rows below.

 It is also the gap a static check cannot close. `check-pipe-verdicts` reads
 `ScExit (SeInt N)` values, so a success reached by termination is invisible to
 it; only a run can put that outcome in front of the model.

Section: Entry

 `PrGuests 1` IS ONE GUEST AND NOT A FAN-OUT, so this pipeline carries no split
 and no join, and the step count says so: four stages, four steps. A predicate
 reading the constructor rather than the count would answer yes here and the
 shape row would go red.

 The four observations, in order: the exit the script really returns; the success
 it really reaches by falling off the end; a code it CANNOT return; and the right
 code observed at the wrong stage. The last two are the discriminators, because a
 runner that explained everything would pass the first two on its own.

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let pd = pipeline-to-process pb-pipe
    in let shape = "steps=" & show (list-length (pd.pd-steps)) & " edges=" & show (list-length (pd.pd-transitions)) & " start=" & (pd.pd-start-step)

    in let a0 = pipe-start pd 10
    in let a1 = pipe-passed pd a0 11
    in let a2 = pipe-passed pd a1 12
    in let a3 = pipe-passed pd a2 13
    in let atBuild = "AT-BUILD at=" & (a3.pi-current-step)

    in let refused = pipe-exited pd a3 2 14
    in let refusedLine = "OBSERVED-2 " & pipe-status-text (refused.pi-status)

    in let ok = pipe-passed pd a3 15
    in let okLine = "OBSERVED-FELL-THROUGH " & pipe-status-text (ok.pi-status) & " at=" & (ok.pi-current-step)

    in let impossible = pipe-exited pd a3 3 16
    in let impossibleLine = "OBSERVED-3 " & pipe-status-text (impossible.pi-status)

    in let wrongStage = pipe-exited pd a1 2 17
    in let wrongStageLine = "OBSERVED-2-AT-PREAMBLE " & pipe-status-text (wrongStage.pi-status)

    in act
      print-line-uni shape
      print-line-uni atBuild
      print-line-uni refusedLine
      print-line-uni okLine
      print-line-uni impossibleLine
      print-line-uni wrongStageLine
      print-line-uni "-- the refused run --"
      print-line-uni (pipe-log-text (refused.pi-history) 0 "")
    end

